### auth_lab.http — Authentication Gauntlet Lab
### Write your REST Client request blocks under each phase header.
### Run a block with Ctrl+Alt+R (Windows/Linux) or Cmd+Alt+R (macOS).
### Hint: all endpoints live under http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 1 — Basic Authentication
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Phase 1 — Basic Auth (unauthenticated)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1

###

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (with credentials)
# REST Client encodes username:password in Base64 automatically if you
# provide credentials, but here we write the raw Authorization header.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=

### Synthesis — Raw header an attacker would reuse
# An attacker who captured the header can replay this exact header line
# to impersonate the admin user without knowing the password:
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=
# This works because Base64 is an encoding, not encryption — it only
# represents the credentials in a reversible form, so anyone with the
# header can resend it and be accepted.


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 3 — Token Authentication (Opaque)
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### Phase 3 — Opaque Token Auth (valid)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/token/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Token c6b044787ebbbe6a6d019aae6de8d58d36238966

###

### Phase 3 — Tampered Token (last char changed 6 → 7)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/token/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Token c6b044787ebbbe6a6d019aae6de8d58d36238967

### Phase 3 — Synthesis: Replay attack with opaque token
# A stolen opaque token can be replayed indefinitely until deleted from the DB.
# Unlike JWT, there is no expiry — only a manual database deletion revokes it.




### Phase 4 — Step 1: Login to obtain JWT access + refresh tokens
# The @name directive lets REST Client capture the response for reuse below.
# @name jwtLogin
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/login/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "username": "admin",
    "password": "admin123"
}

###

### Phase 4 — JWT Protected Request
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/protected/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{jwtLogin.response.body.access}}


###

### Phase 4 — Step 3: Refresh the access token using the refresh token
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/refresh/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "refresh": "{{jwtLogin.response.body.refresh}}"
}

###

### Phase 4 — Synthesis: Tampered JWT
# Steps to create this token:
#   1. Copy the access token from the jwtLogin response.
#   2. Go to jwt.io and paste it into the "Encoded" box.
#   3. In the purple Payload panel, change "user_id" to 2.
#   4. Copy the new Encoded token jwt.io shows on the left.
#   5. Paste it below, replacing <PASTE_TAMPERED_TOKEN_HERE>.
#
# Expected result: 401 Unauthorized — the server rejects it because the
# signature in the token's third segment no longer matches the tampered
# payload. The server recomputes the signature with its SECRET_KEY and
# the two values don't match, proving the token was modified.
### Phase 4 — Synthesis: Tampered JWT
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/protected/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ0b2tlbl90eXBlIjoiYWNjZXNzIiwiZXhwIjoxNzc5ODI2NzMxLCJpYXQiOjE3Nzk4MjQ5MzEsImp0aSI6IjkzYTY3NDE3MmJmMzQxZTlhZWQ4MzNiNWYwZTQ5MzE3IiwidXNlcl9pZCI6IjEifQ.VKsLgH8_q-xDiv1iSX1n8-aRecMJH-vuSvvgY_l5V-X

